{# IN·15 — WHERE THINGS STAND: the state machine for the ONE incident you last
   touched.

   PERSON-SCOPED, and it says so. Two people looking at the same dashboard see two
   different incidents here, which is why the header always NAMES the incident and
   why this is never a summary. Touched nothing today? It falls back to the top of
   your queue and renames its own header rather than rendering an empty card.

   The rail itself is the SAME component the case file draws — identical markup,
   identical guards. #}
{% import '@UhifadhiLabsIncident/dashboard/_ui.html.twig' as ui %}
<div class="c" data-w="rail">
    <span class="tab"><span class="idx">IN·15</span>Where things stand<span class="src">· {{ dashboard.rail ? dashboard.rail.reason : 'nothing is on your desk' }}</span></span>
    {% if dashboard.rail %}
        {% set incident = dashboard.rail.incident %}
        <div class="i-railhd">
            <a class="i-railid" href="{{ path('incident_show', {uuid: area.uuid, reference: incident.reference}) }}">{{ incident.reference }}</a>
            <span class="i-railttl">{{ incident.title }}</span>
            {{ ui.cat(incident.category, incident.subcategory.label) }}
            {{ ui.status(incident.status) }}
            <span class="i-spacer"></span>
            <a class="open-btn" href="{{ path('incident_show', {uuid: area.uuid, reference: incident.reference}) }}">Open the case →</a>
        </div>
        {{ include('@UhifadhiLabsIncident/dashboard/_rail.html.twig', {
            area: area,
            rail: dashboard.rail,
            canManage: false,
            csrfToken: null,
        }, with_context: false) }}
        <div class="use">The moves are made on the case file, where the whole record is in front of you — this rail says where the thing is, not what to do about it in passing.</div>
    {% else %}
        <div class="use">You have not touched an incident here, and nothing is waiting on you. The widget says so rather than drawing an empty rail.</div>
    {% endif %}
</div>
